course: dictionary
lesson: 7515
title: Poverty: a word with two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Poverty is a noun. Its main meaning is being very poor: not having enough money for food, clothes or a home. «People live longer where there is clean water, enough food, good doctors and peace; poverty and war shorten lives.» Here poverty is a problem that makes people's lives shorter.
explain: Look at the words near poverty for clues. Phrases like live in poverty, extreme poverty, and end poverty show it is a serious problem that people want to stop. «Extreme poverty» means being very, very poor, and «end poverty» means making it stop.
explain: Poverty also has a formal meaning: a lack of something. «A poverty of ideas» means having very few new ideas. Check it by swapping in lack: «a lack of ideas» still makes sense, so you know you have the right meaning.
explain: Some words are used in special ways. «Monks and nuns take vows of poverty, owning nothing of their own.» Here the sentence tells you what poverty means for them. When a word has a meaning in brackets or nearby, use it as your clue.
fact: Poverty means being very poor, without enough money for food, clothes or a home.
fact: Phrases to know: live in poverty, extreme poverty, poverty line, end poverty.
fact: In a formal sense, poverty means a lack of something, as in «poverty of ideas».
fact: «Extreme poverty has fallen from most of humanity around 1820 to about 1 person in 10 today» shows that poverty has fallen a lot, but it has not gone.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple word, such as «very poor» or «lack of», and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «Extreme poverty has fallen from most of humanity around 1820 to about 1 person in 10 today.» What does poverty mean here? | Being rich and very happy | A kind of weather | *Being very poor, without enough money for basic needs | Having lots of spare time
exercise: «Monks and nuns take vows of poverty, owning nothing of their own.» What does poverty mean in this sentence? | Being very ill | Owning a large house | Having too many friends | *Owning nothing of their own
exercise: In the phrase «a poverty of ideas», what does poverty mean? | *A lack of something, here new ideas | A large number | Lots of money | Being very tired
test: What is the best swap for poverty in «poverty and war shorten lives»? | *Being very poor and short of food | Being very happy and healthy | A type of weather | A kind of game
test: In «vows of poverty (owning nothing of their own)», what does the bracket tell you? | It explains prayer | It explains that monks are rich | It explains vows: a promise to marry | *It explains poverty: owning nothing of their own
exam: The sentence «Extreme poverty has fallen... to about 1 person in 10 today» shows progress, so why does the author also say hundreds of millions are still very poor? | *To show that poverty has fallen, but has not gone completely | To show that poverty is a kind of weather | To show that poverty only happens in the 1820s | To show that poverty has grown every year
